# Line endings, pinned per path.
#
# `core.autocrlf` is true on this machine, so without these rules a checkout rewrites every text
# file to CRLF - including the JSON files this repo requires to be LF. That is not cosmetic: two
# tests compare RAW BYTES, and the failure they produce blames content when the cause is the
# checkout. It has bitten twice, most recently when a plain `git checkout Data/Custom/navigation.json`
# brought the file back as CRLF and the golden byte-identity test started failing on a file whose
# records were identical.
#
# See CLAUDE.md, "Line endings are not uniform in this tree".

# --- JSON that must be LF -------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# All of it is written by a program rather than typed: the shard through `AtomicFile.Write`, the
# editor through `compact.js`, and the reference through `tools/nav-import/uo-offline.js`. All
# three emit LF, so LF is what the file is, and `project.test.js` asserts the shipped files are
# byte-for-byte what the writer produces.

Data/Custom/navigation.json          text eol=lf
Data/Custom/britain-daily-life.json  text eol=lf
Data/Custom/restricted-zones.json    text eol=lf
Data/Custom/core-smoke.json          text eol=lf
Data/Custom/golden/*.json            text eol=lf
Data/Custom/reference/*.json         text eol=lf

# `bots.json` is deliberately absent: it is hand-edited, is CRLF today, and nothing compares its
# bytes. Pinning it would renormalise a file for no reason.

# --- Our spawn XML is LF, for the same reason the JSON is ------------------------------------
#
# Nobody types these files either. `GG_BotPop.xml` is written whole by the population generator
# through `AtomicFile.Write`, and the others are round-tripped by the editor's `spawnxml.js`, which
# preserves whatever newline the document already had. All of them are LF in the tree today.
#
# Unpinned, `core.autocrlf` hands a fresh checkout CRLF versions of all three - and then the first
# `[BotPopulationGen` rewrites its file's every line back to LF, producing a thirty-seven record
# diff that reads as a content change and is not one. Same failure the JSON rules above exist to
# prevent, one directory over.
#
# SCOPED TO Spawns/Custom, deliberately, exactly as the C# rule below is scoped: the ~2,500 stock
# spawn files in `Spawns/` are upstream's and are not ours to renormalise.

Spawns/Custom/**/*.xml               text eol=lf

# --- C# stays CRLF --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Which is what it already checks out as. Stated rather than inherited from autocrlf, so a
# checkout on a machine configured differently produces the same tree.
#
# SCOPED TO OUR OWN CODE, deliberately. A bare `*.cs` reaches the whole of upstream ServUO, whose
# blobs were committed containing CRLF - 272 files, every line of each, would be rewritten to
# satisfy the rule. That is a line-ending-only diff across somebody else's tree, and it would make
# those files show as permanently modified until somebody took it. Our own files are the ones we
# write, and the ones a flip would actually cost us.

Scripts/Custom/**/*.cs text eol=crlf

# --- tools/ is LF throughout ------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The editor, its bridge and MapExport. Without this, `core.autocrlf` rewrites all 56 of them to
# CRLF on a fresh checkout - and one of them, `js/compact.js`, is the writer whose byte-for-byte
# output `project.test.js` compares against the shipped JSON. A checkout would break a test about
# content by changing something else entirely, which is the exact failure the rules above exist
# to stop.
#
# NOTHING IS RENORMALISED BY THIS. All 56 tracked files are already LF in both the index and the
# working tree (`git ls-files --eol tools/`), because every one of them was written by a tool
# rather than checked out. The rule records what they are; it does not change them.
#
# NOTE THAT `tools/MapExport/*.cs` BECOMES LF, while `Scripts/Custom/**/*.cs` above stays CRLF.
# That is deliberate rather than an oversight. `tools/` is a self-contained toolchain that is
# already LF end to end, and pinning it to what it is beats renormalising eight C# files to match
# a convention that belongs to the shard's source tree.

tools/**                             text eol=lf

# Insurance rather than a live concern: no binary is tracked under tools/ today (42 .js, 8 .cs,
# 2 .ps1, and one each of .md, .html, .css, .csproj - the rendered tiles are gitignored). A
# screenshot fixture added later would be silently corrupted by the rule above, so it is excluded
# now while the cost is one line.

tools/**/*.png                       -text
tools/**/*.jpg                       -text
tools/**/*.ico                       -text

# --- the licence text, byte for byte ---------------------------------------------------------
#
# LICENSE-GPL3 is the FSF's published gpl-3.0.txt, reproduced unmodified so that anyone who
# receives this work receives the licence with it. LICENSE-BOTS pins its length and SHA-256, and
# a checkout that rewrote its line endings would change both - so the file is what it is, LF.

LICENSE-GPL3                         text eol=lf
